WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.760
You know the Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:08.029
You know their friends, you know their enemies
— but did you notice these cameos in Vol. 3?

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:12.570
While trying to get hold of Rocket's file
on the Orgosphere, Nebula, Star-Lord, and

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.750
Gamora force a record keeper to access it
for them.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.199
Her name is Ura, and she is played by none
other than emerging comic book adaptation

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:22.970
staple Daniela Melchior.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:29.789
"He left out some important information, but
that is the gist of it."

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:34.910
Melchior is relatively new to the Hollywood
scene, but she's already made quite a splash.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:40.340
Before taking on the role of Ura, she proved
to be a standout in the 2021 film The Suicide

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.000
Squad, in which she played Cleo Cazo — better
known as Ratcatcher 2.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.486
Additionally, she voiced Gwen Stacy in the
Portuguese dub of Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse.

00:00:50.486 --> 00:00:54.576
Before the Guardians make it to the record
room, they have to get inside the Orgosphere.

00:00:54.576 --> 00:00:59.320
Unfortunately for them, OrgoCorp doesn't mess
around when it comes to security.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.998
Notably, an overseer named Kwol watches the
various security screens that guard the entire base.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:10.119
Kwol, who is last seen in Guardians Vol. 3
taking a brutal shot to the leg, was played

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:14.020
by James Gunn's wife and frequent collaborator,
Jennifer Holland.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.580
Kwol is actually Holland's second comic book
movie role to date, as she has played Emilia

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.409
Harcourt, an agent of Amanda Waller, in the
DC Universe.

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:27.509
She also appeared in 2019's Brightburn, the
David Yarovesky movie that was co-produced

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:28.509
by Gunn.

00:01:28.994 --> 00:01:33.220
The Guardians' base of operations, Knowhere,
is an odd place, to say the least.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:38.800
Situated inside the head of a dead Celestial,
it was previously run by Taneleer Tivan, otherwise

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.189
known as the Collector.

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:44.500
The streets of Knowhere have been cleaned
up since then, but it's not perfect.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.350
For example, Knowhere is still home to some
strange and seedy individuals by the time

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:53.120
of Guardians Vol. 3 — including none other
than Seth Green's Howard the Duck.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.479
Howard can be seen playing a card game with
a number of other characters, including Cosmo

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:58.760
and Kraglin.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.500
Green has voiced the humanoid duck in both
the Guardians of the Galaxy movies and the

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.219
animated Disney+ series What If…?

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.085
"You know what they say, when you're out of
luck, always go duck."

00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:11.846
"I'm pretty sure no one says that."

00:02:11.846 --> 00:02:13.020
"Oh, they say it."

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:13.520
"Really?"

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.520
"Totally."

00:02:14.632 --> 00:02:20.063
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3's villain,
the High Evolutionary, is a truly twisted individual.

00:02:20.063 --> 00:02:25.031
In his pursuit of building the perfect world,
he has created some truly monstrous beings.

00:02:25.031 --> 00:02:30.180
Take the weaponized juggernauts Behemoth and
War Pig, for example: a cyborg bird and pig

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.540
who are tasked with protecting the High Evolutionary
and carrying out his wishes.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:40.158
Reinaldo Faberlle provides the voice of Behemoth,
and, of all people, Judy Greer voiced War Pig.

00:02:40.158 --> 00:02:45.080
Greer is no stranger to the MCU, of course,
as she played the ex-wife of Scott Lang in

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.610
the Ant-Man franchise.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.140
Outside of the MCU, Greer has appeared in
everything from Jurassic World to the latest

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.140
Halloween trilogy.

00:02:52.871 --> 00:02:58.790
In Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3, Cosmo and
Kraglin find themselves at odds over a petty squabble.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:03.379
He calls her a bad dog, and she refuses to
stop pestering him about taking it back, which

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:04.849
he's adamant he won't do.

00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:09.049
Their ongoing feud eventually gets on the
nerves of their fellow Knowhere residents.

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:13.629
One of the individuals who's sick of hearing
about their animosity is the Broker, as played

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:15.590
by Christopher Fairbank.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.900
Guardians of the Galaxy fans will likely remember
him from the first installment in the trilogy,

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.250
in which Peter Quill attempts to sell him
the Orb.

00:03:22.997 --> 00:03:24.905
"You got the best eyebrows in the business."

00:03:25.607 --> 00:03:30.599
Evidently, the Broker survived the destruction
of Xandar by Thanos' forces and has since

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:32.409
started a new life on Knowhere.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.980
Cosmo and Kraglin's feud annoys yet another
notable individual in their card game: Bzermikitokolok,

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:43.459
the denizen of Knowhere, who makes his big
screen debut in Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.

00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:45.560
3 courtesy of Rhett Miller.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.970
Miller has been the frontman and lead singer
of the country rock band Old 97's since the

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.250
group formed back in 1992.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.799
Miller showed off his musical prowess in his
first MCU appearance, at the beginning and

00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:59.880
end of The Guardians of the Galaxy Holiday
Special.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:05.099
The Disney+ special sees Bzermikitokolok and
his band, Bzermikitokolok and the Knowheremen,

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:07.970
work hard to bring some Christmas cheer to
Knowhere.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:12.800
Unfortunately, they woefully misunderstand
basically every aspect of the holiday.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:16.399
"That is not part of the lore at all."

00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:21.549
Pete Davidson and James Gunn have worked together
before, as DC fans will likely remember; he

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:23.800
played Blackguard in The Suicide Squad.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.830
For Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3, the pair
reunited again — albeit briefly, and in

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:32.960
a way that only die-hard fans of the comedian
are likely to recognize.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:37.630
Davidson plays a character called Phlektik,
an alien guard of the High Evolutionary.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:42.310
Because Phlektik is a member of an undisclosed
alien species, it's obviously very difficult

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.270
to tell that Davison is there.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:49.120
However, as Gunn noted on Twitter, the actor
very much put in the work, as Davidson both

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.856
voiced the character and physically portrayed
him.

00:04:51.856 --> 00:04:52.856
Gunn wrote:

00:04:52.856 --> 00:04:58.080
"And my friend Pete not only voiced but actually
came to visit us in Atlanta for a day and

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.694
played Phlektik on set."

00:05:00.276 --> 00:05:02.639
Who doesn't love a good creator cameo?

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:07.539
Still, you'd be forgiven for missing James
Gunn's part in Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:11.580
3, especially considering his character is
anything but recognizable.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:17.030
In fact, it seems that Gunn chose to portray
the strangest minor character in the movie.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:21.300
Gunn himself confirmed on Twitter that he
provided the voice of Lambshank, the unsettling

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.980
yet polite scientific experiment encountered
by Mantis when she frees the High Evolutionary's

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.231
prisoners at the end of the film.

00:05:28.231 --> 00:05:33.460
Eagly the eagle is the lovable CGI bird of
prey that appears prominently in James Gunn's

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.740
DCEU show Peacemaker.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.520
As it happens, a very similar-looking bird
turns up as one of the caged animals on the

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.580
High Evolutionary's ship in Guardians of the
Galaxy Vol. 3.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:47.460
When Gunn took a moment to answer some fan
questions on Twitter, one viewer took the

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.221
opportunity to question whether the two birds'
similarities were intentional.

00:05:52.221 --> 00:05:53.221
Gunn confirmed:

00:05:53.221 --> 00:05:55.221
"That was actually the real Eagly."

00:05:55.430 --> 00:06:00.970
In almost any other occasion, the surprise
presence of a DCEU character in a MCU movie

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:02.740
would be monumental news.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.572
Then again, sometimes a bird is just a bird.

